    .page-content
      h1 Documentation
      p How GNIS-LD identifiers are structured, how the dataset is versioned, and how to discover and query it with standard dataset metadata. For hands-on query recipes, see the #[a(href='/integrations') integrations guide].

      h2#uris URI structure
      p Every GNIS-LD URI is under #[code http://#{base_url}/lod/]. Each URI is a permanent identifier that also works as a URL. A URI never changes, and every one dereferences to the record it identifies.
      table.doc-table
        thead
          tr
            th URI pattern
            th Identifies
        tbody
          tr
            td: code /lod/gnis/feature/{id}
            td A named feature. #[code {id}] is the official USGS GNIS Feature ID. For example, #[a(href='/lod/gnis/feature/1414314') feature/1414314] is Denali, and its id stayed #[code 1414314] when the official name changed to Mount McKinley in 2025.
          tr
            td: code /lod/gnis/feature-alias/{State.Name}
            td A human-readable alias for states and counties, such as #[code Alaska.Juneau].
          tr
            td: code /lod/gnis/ontology/{term}
            td A term in the #[code gnis] vocabulary (properties such as #[code officialName], #[code county], #[code description]).
          tr
            td: code /lod/usgs/ontology/{term}
            td A term in the #[code usgs] vocabulary (feature classes such as #[code Summit], #[code Stream], #[code Dam]).
          tr
            td: code /lod/geometry/{type}/{id}
            td A geometry, where #[code {type}] is one of #[code point], #[code multipoint], #[code linestring], #[code multilinestring], #[code polygon], #[code multipolygon].
      p Additional namespaces under #[code /lod/] (#[code nhd], #[code cegis]) are reserved for sibling USGS datasets that share this infrastructure.
      p Every #[code /lod/] URI supports content negotiation. Request HTML in a browser, or RDF from a client:
      .code-card
        pre.
          <span class="cm">$</span> curl -H <span class="str">'Accept: text/turtle'</span> https://#{base_url}/lod/gnis/feature/1414314

      h2#properties What a feature record contains
      p The properties of a feature record are listed below. Values marked as entities are themselves dereferenceable URIs.
      table.doc-table
        thead
          tr
            th Property
            th Value
            th Description
        tbody
          tr
            td: code rdf:type
            td entity
            td One or more feature classes from the #[code usgs] vocabulary (#[code usgs:Summit], #[code usgs:Stream], …).
          tr
            td: code gnis:officialName
            td string
            td The federally recognized name of the feature. #[code rdfs:label] has the same value, so tools that display #[code rdfs:label] show the official name.
          tr
            td: code gnis:alternativeName
            td string
            td Variant names and historical spellings, when they exist.
          tr
            td: code gnis:description
            td string
            td A prose description of the feature.
          tr
            td: code gnis:history
            td string
            td Historical notes, such as the origin of the name and changes over time.
          tr
            td: code gnis:citation
            td string
            td The source citation for the record.
          tr
            td: code gnis:featureId
            td string
            td The numeric USGS GNIS Feature ID (the same id that appears in the URI).
          tr
            td: code gnis:county
            td entity
            td The county the feature is in, a #[code feature-alias] URI that has #[code gnis:countyName] and #[code gnis:countyCode].
          tr
            td: code gnis:state
            td entity
            td The state, a #[code feature-alias] URI that has #[code gnis:stateName], #[code gnis:stateCode], and #[code gnis:stateId].
          tr
            td: code gnis:mapName
            td string
            td The USGS topographic map the feature appears on.
          tr
            td: code gnis:dateFeatureCreated,
              br
              code gnis:dateFeatureEdited,
              br
              code gnis:dateNameCreated
            td date
            td When the record was created and last edited upstream, and when the name was established.
          tr
            td: code geo:hasGeometry
            td entity
            td The feature's geometry under #[code /lod/geometry/], queryable with GeoSPARQL (WKT via #[code geo:asWKT]).
          tr
            td: code owl:sameAs,
              br
              code skos:closeMatch
            td entity
            td Crosswalk links to Wikidata, DBpedia, GeoNames, Who's on First, and Google Data Commons (#[code owl:sameAs]), plus Library of Congress and Getty TGN authorities (#[code skos:closeMatch]). They are harvested for each release from Wikidata's GNIS crosswalk, a name-and-distance alignment against the GeoNames gazetteer, and the Census GEOID join.
          tr
            td: code gnis:censusGeoid
            td string
            td The U.S. Census Bureau GEOID, joined from the Census national gazetteer (whose ANSI code #[em is] the GNIS Feature ID). Counties and populated places have it. It is the join key for Census statistical products.
      p Not every feature has every property. Descriptions and histories in particular are present only where USGS recorded them. The #[a(href='/explore') explore workspace] shows a real record. Run the Denali example and every property above appears in the result.

      h2#howto-link How-to: link an entity into your dataset
      p This is the main use of GNIS-LD. Take a place mentioned in your data, attach its stable USGS identifier, and get authoritative geographic context back.

      h3 1. Find the identifier
      p For a single name, use the #[a(href='/search') search page]. Type the name, filter if needed, and copy the URI from the result card. For bulk lookups, run an exact-match query once per name:
      .code-card
        .code-card-head
          span.dot
          span.dot
          span.dot
          span.code-card-name resolve.rq
        pre.
          <span class="kw">PREFIX</span> rdf: &lt;http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#&gt;
          <span class="kw">PREFIX</span> rdfs: &lt;http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#&gt;
          <span class="kw">PREFIX</span> gnis: &lt;http://#{base_url}/lod/gnis/ontology/&gt;

          <span class="kw">SELECT</span> ?feature ?type <span class="kw">WHERE</span> {
            ?feature (gnis:officialName|rdfs:label) ?label .
            <span class="kw">FILTER</span>(LCASE(STR(?label)) = <span class="str">"denali"</span>)
            ?feature rdf:type ?type .
          }
      p Many features share a name. Disambiguate with the feature type (#[code rdf:type]) and the state (#[code gnis:state]) before choosing a URI.

      h3 2. Store the URI in your data
      p Store the whole URI, never just the numeric id. In RDF, use the feature URI as the object of a spatial property:
      .code-card
        .code-card-head
          span.dot
          span.dot
          span.dot
          span.code-card-name your-data.ttl
        pre.
          @prefix dct: &lt;http://purl.org/dc/terms/&gt; .

          &lt;https://example.org/observation/42&gt;
              dct:spatial &lt;http://#{base_url}/lod/gnis/feature/1414314&gt; .
      p Without RDF, a plain #[code gnis_uri] column in a table or a string field in JSON works as well. Either way the URI is permanent and identifies the feature without any other context.

      h3 3. Get value back
      p Once the link exists, the federal record is one hop away. Dereference the URI for the authoritative name and full record, or join your data against the live endpoint in a single federated query:
      .code-card
        .code-card-head
          span.dot
          span.dot
          span.dot
          span.code-card-name enrich.rq
        pre.
          <span class="kw">PREFIX</span> dct: &lt;http://purl.org/dc/terms/&gt;
          <span class="kw">PREFIX</span> gnis: &lt;http://#{base_url}/lod/gnis/ontology/&gt;

          <span class="kw">SELECT</span> ?obs ?name ?county <span class="kw">WHERE</span> {
            ?obs dct:spatial ?feature .
            <span class="kw">SERVICE</span> &lt;https://#{base_url}/sparql&gt; {
              ?feature gnis:officialName ?name ;
                       gnis:county ?county .
            }
          }
      p Your dataset is not copied. Names, counties, and the rest of the #[a(href='#properties') feature record] are read from the authoritative source at query time. More patterns are in the #[a(href='/integrations') integrations guide].

      h2#crosswalks Crosswalks to other systems
      p Every release contains over two million crosswalk links, so a GNIS identifier also links a feature to its matching records in the datasets listed below. The links are derived automatically for each release from Wikidata's GNIS crosswalk (P590), GNIS-LD's own alignment against the GeoNames gazetteer, and the Census national gazetteer, whose ANSI code #[em is] the GNIS Feature ID. The GeoNames alignment is generated by GNIS-LD and scored against Wikidata's curated pairs as a QA step on every build.
      table.doc-table
        thead
          tr
            th Target
            th Property
            th Derived from
            th Links
        tbody
          tr
            td GeoNames
            td: code owl:sameAs
            td Name-and-distance alignment, QA-scored against Wikidata's curated pairs (96.7% agreement)
            td ≈ 920,000
          tr
            td Wikidata
            td: code owl:sameAs
            td P590 backlinks
            td ≈ 880,000
          tr
            td DBpedia
            td: code owl:sameAs
            td English Wikipedia sitelinks
            td ≈ 165,000
          tr
            td Who's on First
            td: code owl:sameAs
            td Wikidata P6766
            td ≈ 90,000
          tr
            td Library of Congress
            td: code skos:closeMatch
            td Wikidata P244
            td ≈ 45,000
          tr
            td Getty TGN
            td: code skos:closeMatch
            td Wikidata P1667
            td ≈ 36,000
          tr
            td Google Data Commons
            td: code owl:sameAs
            td Census GEOID (#[code geoId/&lt;FIPS&gt;])
            td ≈ 36,000
          tr
            td Census Bureau
            td: code gnis:censusGeoid
            td National gazetteer ANSI join
            td ≈ 36,000
      p Following the links outward is one query. So is arriving from another system's identifier:
      .code-card
        .code-card-head
          span.dot
          span.dot
          span.dot
          span.code-card-name crosswalk.rq
        pre.
          <span class="kw">PREFIX</span> owl: &lt;http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#&gt;
          <span class="kw">PREFIX</span> skos: &lt;http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#&gt;
          <span class="kw">PREFIX</span> gnisf: &lt;http://#{base_url}/lod/gnis/feature/&gt;
          <span class="kw">PREFIX</span> wd: &lt;http://www.wikidata.org/entity/&gt;

          <span class="cm"># everywhere else a feature is known</span>
          <span class="kw">SELECT</span> ?elsewhere <span class="kw">WHERE</span> { gnisf:1414314 (owl:sameAs|skos:closeMatch) ?elsewhere }

          <span class="cm"># from a Wikidata id back to the GNIS feature</span>
          <span class="kw">SELECT</span> ?feature <span class="kw">WHERE</span> { ?feature owl:sameAs wd:Q130018 }

      h2#versioning Identifier stability &amp; versioning
      p GNIS-LD makes two promises about change over time:
      p #[strong Identifiers are permanent.] Feature URIs are built on USGS GNIS Feature IDs, which are never reused or reassigned. A URI minted for a feature keeps identifying that feature forever. If USGS removes a record from its products, its last-known triples are kept in the #[em historical companion] file published with every later release on the #[a(href='/downloads') downloads page], and they stay loaded in the live endpoint. They are typed #[code usgs:HistoricalFeature] with #[code gnis:lastAppearedIn] pointing at the release that last contained the feature as a current record. A feature that USGS adds back to its products is a current record again and leaves the historical set. A #[code feature-alias] URI that disambiguates several features is kept only when every one of them has been removed. A GNIS-LD URI stored in your own systems as a foreign key therefore keeps resolving to the same feature.
      p #[strong The graph is loaded from each upstream GNIS release.] The live endpoint always serves the current release, loaded from the USGS source data. A release is identified by the dataset-level metadata described below, and every release can be downloaded from the #[a(href='/downloads') downloads] page.

      h2#sources Source datasets
      p GNIS-LD is derived from federal source data. These are the places to go to understand where the records come from, how they are maintained, and how they are converted:
      .list
        .item
          .title
            a(href='https://www.usgs.gov/tools/geographic-names-information-system-gnis' target='_blank') Geographic Names Information System (GNIS)
          .info The primary source. It is the federal and national standard for geographic nomenclature, developed by USGS.
        .item
          .title
            a(href='https://www.usgs.gov/us-board-on-geographic-names' target='_blank') U.S. Board on Geographic Names
          .info The federal body that establishes and maintains uniform geographic name usage. GNIS is its official repository.
        .item
          .title
            a(href='https://www.usgs.gov/us-board-on-geographic-names/download-gnis-data' target='_blank') GNIS data downloads
          .info The raw files GNIS-LD is built from, published by USGS.
        .item
          .title
            a(href='https://github.com/DataONEorg/usgs-triplifier' target='_blank') usgs-triplifier
          .info The open-source pipeline that converts the USGS files into the RDF served here.
      p The reserved #[code nhd] and #[code cegis] namespaces correspond to the #[a(href='https://www.usgs.gov/national-hydrography/national-hydrography-dataset' target='_blank') National Hydrography Dataset] and USGS #[a(href='https://www.usgs.gov/centers/cegis' target='_blank') CEGIS] respectively.

      h2#known-issues Known data issues

      h3 Current USGS products
      p #[strong Features without coordinates.] USGS lists about 12,500 features at latitude 0 and longitude 0, which marks their location as unknown. GNIS-LD gives these features no geometry, so a spatial query leaves them out when it requires one:
      .code-card
        pre.
          ?feature geo:hasGeometry ?geometry .
      p #[strong Unknown citations.] When USGS recorded no source for an official name, #[code gnis:citation] is the IRI #[code gnis:UnknownCitation] instead of a text literal. Add #[code FILTER(isLiteral(?citation))] to get only real citations.
      p #[strong Mexican states.] The USGS government units include six Mexican states, typed #[code gnis:State] like U.S. states. Their #[code gnis:stateCode] has three letters: BCN, SON, CHH, COA, NLE and TAM. Add #[code FILTER(STRLEN(?code) = 2)] to get only U.S. states and territories.
      p #[strong Independent cities in the Census gazetteers.] Virginia's 38 independent cities, Baltimore, St. Louis and Carson City are each listed in both the Census county gazetteer and the place gazetteer under the same GNIS id, with a different GEOID in each. GNIS-LD links each of them to its county GEOID only. For example, Virginia Beach has #[code gnis:censusGeoid "51810"], and its place GEOID 5182000 is not linked.
      p #[strong A Census code shared by two places.] The 2025 Census place gazetteer gives Colonial Forge CDP and Embrey Mill CDP, both in Virginia, the same GNIS id, 2832523. That id belongs to Colonial Forge. GNIS-LD cannot tell from the gazetteer which row is correct, so #[a(href='/lod/gnis/feature/2832523') feature/2832523] has no Census link.
      p #[strong No elevation.] Current releases have no #[code gnis:elevation]. See #[a(href='#faq') the FAQ] for why and where to get elevations.

      h3 Legacy dumps (through August 2021)
      p Releases built from USGS legacy dumps, such as release 210825, and historical features copied from those releases come from the file format USGS used until 2021. That format has errors that the current products have since corrected.
      p #[strong Several states and counties per feature.] The legacy file lists a feature once for each state and county it lies in, and each row adds a #[code gnis:state] and a #[code gnis:county] to the feature. The 2021 dump has 45 such features, on a state line or on the Canadian or Mexican border. Their state and county combinations include some that do not exist. For example, Spaulding Pond (#[a(href='/lod/gnis/feature/575984') feature/575984]) on the Maine and New Hampshire line has the counties #[code Maine.Strafford], #[code Maine.York], #[code New_Hampshire.Strafford] and #[code New_Hampshire.York], although Strafford County is in New Hampshire and York County is in Maine. The current products list each of these features in one state and county. Every row of such a feature has the same elevation, so the feature has one #[code gnis:elevation].
      p #[strong Canadian provinces and Mexican states as codes.] A feature that crosses the Canadian or Mexican border has a #[code gnis:state] for its U.S. state and one built from the province or state code, such as #[code feature-alias/AB] for Alberta and #[code feature-alias/SON] for Sonora.
      p #[strong Alaska features listed under Arizona.] The 2021 dump lists 52 features in Alaska's Chugach Census Area twice, once under Alaska and once under Arizona. Both rows have Alaska's state number, 02, so the Arizona row is a USGS error. GNIS-LD skips a legacy row whose state code does not match its state number, so these features are listed under Alaska only, as in the current products. Sixteen of them, airports and heliports that are no longer in the current products, are historical features built from these rows.

      h2#metadata Dataset metadata (VoID &amp; DCAT)
      p The dataset is described by a machine-readable #[a(href='http://rdfs.org/ns/void#' target='_blank') VoID] and #[a(href='https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-dcat-2/' target='_blank') DCAT] document at #[a(href='/resource/metadata/void.ttl') /resource/metadata/void.ttl] (also reachable at the VoID well-known location, #[code /.well-known/void]). The document records the SPARQL endpoint, URI space, vocabularies, triple count, license, and the bulk distribution:
      .code-card
        pre.
          <span class="cm">$</span> curl https://#{base_url}/.well-known/void

      h2#lineage Querying across lineage
      p The abstract dataset is identified as #[code &lt;http://#{base_url}/lod/gnis&gt;]. Dated release records point to it with #[code dct:isVersionOf], so the release lineage is queried with SPARQL over the metadata. For example, the following SPARQL lists the releases with their issue dates and distributions:
      .code-card
        .code-card-head
          span.dot
          span.dot
          span.dot
          span.code-card-name lineage.rq
        pre.
          <span class="kw">PREFIX</span> dct: &lt;http://purl.org/dc/terms/&gt;
          <span class="kw">PREFIX</span> dcat: &lt;http://www.w3.org/ns/dcat#&gt;

          <span class="kw">SELECT</span> ?release ?issued ?distribution <span class="kw">WHERE</span> {
            ?release dct:isVersionOf &lt;http://#{base_url}/lod/gnis&gt; ;
                     dct:issued ?issued .
            <span class="kw">OPTIONAL</span> { ?release dcat:distribution ?distribution . }
          }
          <span class="kw">ORDER BY</span> <span class="kw">DESC</span>(?issued)

      h2#faq Frequently asked questions
      .faq
        details.faq-item
          summary How long are my URIs valid for?
          .faq-body
            p Forever. Feature URIs are built on USGS GNIS Feature IDs, which are never reused or reassigned. If USGS removes a feature from its products, its last-known record is retained. It is typed #[code usgs:HistoricalFeature], with #[code gnis:lastAppearedIn] linking to the release it last appeared in.
        details.faq-item
          summary How do I query across datasets?
          .faq-body
            p Use SPARQL federation by adding a #[code SERVICE &lt;https://#{base_url}/sparql&gt;] clause to a query running in your own store. The #[a(href='/integrations') integrations guide] shows the pattern, and the #[a(href='#howto-link') how-to above] walks through enriching linked records.
        details.faq-item
          summary How often is the data updated?
          .faq-body
            p Quarterly, after each USGS GNIS domestic names release.
        details.faq-item
          summary Can I use the data commercially?
          .faq-body
            p Yes. GNIS is US federal government data in the public domain. There are no licensing restrictions on use, redistribution, or commercial products. Citing GNIS-LD and the USGS as the source is appreciated but not required.
        details.faq-item
          summary Where did the elevation data go?
          .faq-body
            p The elevations in GNIS were 3DEP-derived values that USGS distributed in the GNIS dumps through 2025. USGS no longer distributes them, so current releases do not include them. The #[code gnis:elevation] property remains in the ontology for data loaded from older dumps, and its #[code rdfs:comment] states that USGS no longer distributes elevation with GNIS. For authoritative elevations, query the USGS National Map elevation services directly using the coordinates from a feature's geometry.
        details.faq-item
          summary I found an error in the data. Where do I report it?
          .faq-body
            p Problems with the GNIS-LD service or the RDF conversion belong on our #[a(href='https://github.com/DataONEorg/gnis-ld.org/issues' target='_blank') GitHub issue tracker]. Errors in the underlying names or records come from the source data and should be reported to the #[a(href='https://www.usgs.gov/us-board-on-geographic-names' target='_blank') U.S. Board on Geographic Names].

  script.
    (function() {
      var links = Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('.toc-link'));
      var sections = links.map(function(l) { return document.getElementById(l.getAttribute('href').slice(1)); });
      function update() {
        var y = window.scrollY + 100;
        var active = 0;
        sections.forEach(function(s, i) { if (s && s.offsetTop <= y) active = i; });
        // when the page is scrolled to the bottom the last section is marked
        // active even if its heading cannot reach the top of the viewport
        if (window.scrollY + window.innerHeight >= document.documentElement.scrollHeight - 5) {
          active = links.length - 1;
        }
        links.forEach(function(l, i) { l.classList.toggle('active', i === active); });
      }
      window.addEventListener('scroll', update, {passive: true});
      update();
    })();
